  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms move in \nthis evening as a cold front sweeps through. A Slight Risk for \nsevere weather is in place for much of the region. \n\nA cold front will continue to move across Pennsylvania and New \nYork through this afternoon and tonight. The front is encountering\na warm and unstable airmass, with showers and thunderstorms \nhaving developed well off to our west across western Pennsylvania.\nThese storms likely will congeal and form a broken line segment\nor several broken line segments as they move toward our area \nthis evening. The main cluster of storms will move into our area\nlikely no earlier than 5 to 6 PM over the Lehigh Valley and \nSouthern Poconos. This cluster of storms likely will push through\nthe I-95 corridor between 8 to 11 PM and then offshore by early\ntomorrow morning. A few rouge showers ahead of the main band are\npossible. \n\nWith steep low level lapse rates of 7.5 to 8 degrees C/km, strong\ndowndrafts/gusty winds are likely to accompany some of the thunderstorms.\nA secondary threat with these storms also is the potential for 1\"\nhail in stronger storms. The Slight risk area was adjusted S/E from\nthe previous 06Z outlook and now covers most areas, except central/southern\nDelmarva and S/E most NJ. Marginal risk exists for the non-slight\nareas. \n\nThe daytime models are becoming more consistent with regards to\nthe activity more concentrated to the N/NW areas with scattered\nareas S/E. The 12Z NAM model stepped back up with more coverage\nafter a dearth of activity on the 00Z run. We'll monitor the \nevolution of the developing convection. Stay tuned for possible \nWatches and Warnings late this afternoon and into the evening. \n\nNo flooding concerns as QPF amounts generally will be around a\ntenth to three tenths of an inch with locally higher amounts\npossible in any thunderstorms. Convection should be fast moving\nas well. \n\nAs the initial front goes through, things should dry out pretty\nquickly. Cannot rule out some patchy fog though in areas that\nsee some rainfall. The drier airmass will hold off until later \non Sunday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry conditions expected through the middle of \nnext week with warming temperatures. \n\nThe region will remain embedded within a belt of somewhat enhanced \nmid-level flow through early next week as sharp trough located over \nNew England begins to close off and track slowly eastward away from \nthe region. Thereafter, mid-level ridging will likely begin to take \nshape across the region through late week. At the surface, a cold \nfront will shift southeastward through the region late Sunday into \nMonday. In its wake, high pressure will shift into the area, and \ngenerally remain in place, though it may begin to break down some by \nlate week.  \n\nThe cold front late Sunday into Monday looks to usher in slightly \ncooler temperatures, with high temperatures on Monday generally in \nthe upper 70s to low 80s, and temperatures overnight Monday night \nfalling into the low to mid 50s. The cool down does look to be \nfairly short lived with the high pressure taking shape across the \narea afterwards. Steady warming is expected Tuesday and beyond, with \nwidespread low 90s by Wednesday and perhaps even mid or upper 90s by \nlate week. \n\nIn terms of precipitation chances, dry conditions are expected through\nTuesday night. Thereafter, as the high pressure breaks down slightly,\nisolated to scattered, primarily diurnally driven convection\nwill be possible mid-late week. For now, PoPs are generally in\nthe 20-40% range each day Wednesday through Saturday. The\ngreatest coverage/POPs appears to be Thursday attm.",
